WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.034
-Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:04.003
I'm here with my co-host,
Dave Grohl!

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.738
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:08.474
♪♪

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.977
You can see new episodes
of his series,

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:15.481
"From Cradle to Stage,"
every Thursday on Paramount+.

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:16.850
I lost my voice.
This is what happens when

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.617
you hang out with Dave Grohl.
-Yep.

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:20.553
-Yeah.
-One of the things that happens.

00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:22.421
-I love co-hosting
the show with you.

00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:23.990
I think this is fun.
-This is amazing.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.893
I can't -- I'm so happy that you
are letting me share your desk.

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.427
-Well, I -- We have, like --

00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:30.596
This is our first time
we have more than 50 people.

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:32.165
We have 100 people
in our audience tonight.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.270
-It's coming back.
-We are coming back.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.172
-Coming back.
-I know it.

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:42.241
-Bringing one of our
favorite acts to come --

00:00:42.241 --> 00:00:44.477
I know it's a big audience.
When was the last time

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:48.547
that you ever played
in front of 100 people?

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:50.517
-Uh, like, 1987.
-Yeah, exactly, yeah.

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:52.117
-Something like that.
Long time ago.

00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:53.486
-Do you remember that?

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:54.988
Like, when -- First
starting out and getting --

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:56.289
Like, 100 people
was a lot of people.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:58.090
-Yeah, I mean, when I first
started out, you know,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.825
I started out in neighborhood
bands and stuff like that.

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:01.961
And then I joined a band --
a punk-rock band

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:04.664
that actually toured
the country and Europe

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:08.467
when I was, like, 17 years old,
so I started touring in vans --

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.603
little vans and playing,
like, little venues

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:13.205
and stuff like that
when I was, like, 17 or 18.

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:15.307
And so that was kind of where
I, like, cut my teeth

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.676
and learned how to do it.

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:19.411
And when I joined Nirvana,
it was sort of the same way.

00:01:19.411 --> 00:01:20.780
Like, we were in a van

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.649
playing small places
that held 100 or 200 people.

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:24.450
And then the band got
really big really quick.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.953
And so -- But, I mean,
we used to play --

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.320
When I first joined Nirvana,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.589
we would play places
a tenth the size of this place.

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:32.325
And then it just
kind of went -- pyew!

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.260
-Did you freak out when people
know the words to your song

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.129
and are singing it back to you
and singing it with you?

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:37.997
-Yeah, I mean,
that's kind of the best part

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.866
of being in a band.
-Really.

00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:41.768
-Where you're in front
of an audience

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.569
and they're singing
along with you,

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.405
and it becomes this really
beautiful communal moment

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:48.140
that, you know --
that everybody is connected.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.642
That's the best part.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:51.377
And the bigger the audience,
the better when that happens.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:52.545
-Yeah, because
they're all doing it.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:53.946
-It's amazing.

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:56.215
-You have a new documentary
out called "What Drives Us"

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:57.917
on Amazon, which is cool.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.753
They have a special thing
on Amazon video. It's Coda.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.321
-Yeah.
-A Coda Collection.

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:03.656
So, it's for musicians,

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.991
people that love music,
want to support music.

00:02:05.991 --> 00:02:10.563
It's about vans and whatever,
a tour bus, or whatever --

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:12.231
whatever drove you to the gig.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:13.666
-Yeah.
-Whether it be a, you know,

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:17.270
whatever, a Nissan Maxima
or whatever vehicle you had.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.704
I love that.
-You had to squeeze

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.372
all of your friends
and all your stuff

00:02:20.372 --> 00:02:21.674
into this one little thing

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.877
so that you could take
your music to another place

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.277
and share it with more people.
-Yeah.

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:26.912
-And the funny thing is,
in that documentary,

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:28.615
that you see that,
like, you know,

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.182
Beatles did the same thing.

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:31.751
So I interviewed younger bands
that are, like,

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:33.186
stuffing all this equipment

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.021
into a tiny van
and sleeping in it.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.390
And then I'm interviewing, like,
Ringo Starr from the Beatles,

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.926
and he's talking about how
the Beatles did the same thing.

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.727
Everybody kind of
does it the same way.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.496
The Beatles' fart in their --

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:46.932
I got fart stories from
Ringo Starr about the Beatles.

00:02:46.932 --> 00:02:48.634
-In the -- Really?
-Yes.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.203
-They don't.
-They did.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:51.837
They do.
-They don't now.

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:53.539
-I think they may.
-They don't now.

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:54.841
-Still.
-Absolutely not.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.209
-Yes. It's true.
-Is it odd to say

00:02:57.209 --> 00:02:59.078
that you're friends
with the Beatles?

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.546
-Yes!
-Does it feel weird

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:02.248
or you're just --
-Absolutely.

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:05.218
Well, we first --
I mean, I grew --

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.952
That's how I learned
how to play music.

00:03:06.952 --> 00:03:08.587
I had a Beatles songbook
and a guitar.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.657
And I would just listen
to the records and do that.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:14.260
So the first time you --
you meet one of them,

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.729
it's just the one-dimensional
becomes the three-dimensional

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:18.464
and you kind of
freak out a little bit.

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:20.433
But then over time, like,
if you become friends

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:24.103
and you jam with each other,
you know, it never wears off.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:27.640
I mean, there was one time
when I had --

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.343
My third child, Ophelia,
she was born,

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.411
and Paul was in town with Nancy,

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:35.781
and they said, "Hey, we want
to come over and see the baby."

00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:37.583
So they came over
to the house and --

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:40.152
I mean, the first thing I did
was hide all the Beatles stuff.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:41.254
Like, you never know.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.122
You never know
how much Beatles stuff

00:03:43.122 --> 00:03:45.191
you have in your house
until a Beatle comes over.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.126
-It gets embarrassing.
-You're just like, "Oh, my God."

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.628
-"Tonight Show" mugs.
-Yeah, it's crazy.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:50.697
And so he came over,
and we hung out,

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:53.665
and there was a piano
in our living room,

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:55.902
and as he was leaving,
it's like he couldn't resist.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.336
And he just sat down
and he started playing

00:03:57.336 --> 00:04:00.673
"Lady Madonna" on the piano
in my living room.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:03.142
And you're just -- I mean,
it's blowing your mind.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.777
And then my daughter, Harper,

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.546
who was, like, 4 years old
at the time,

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.548
she runs into the kitchen,
she gets a coffee cup,

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:09.983
she fills it
full of spare change

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:11.784
and puts it on the piano
like a tip jar.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.953
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.422
-That's a good bit.
That's a good bit.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:18.191
-That was cool.
-Did he love it? He loved it.

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:19.659
-Absolutely. It was amazing.

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:22.395
-That's kind of the fun part
about all the giant bands,

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:24.596
I want to say,
is that their senses of humor --

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:26.665
They have a sense of humor,
and they're fun.

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:28.300
They're great on stage.
They're great musicians.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.270
But they're fun, and
they're funny and they laugh.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.104
And I feel like
you have that, too.

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:33.539
-Well, you have to imagine --

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.408
Like, you know,
you can't take it too ser--

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:38.043
I mean, anybody that's seen,
like, a Foo Fighters video

00:04:38.043 --> 00:04:41.213
or, you know -- or this
that we're doing right now.

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:43.115
-Yeah.
-Like, you know, you kind of --

00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:45.751
You have to --
You have to be real

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:49.188
and be true and passionate,
and you have to honor the craft

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:51.256
of making music,
but then everything else,

00:04:51.256 --> 00:04:52.925
you just kind of have to
take with a grain of salt.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.894
-You didn't want
to really practice.

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.628
You didn't want to rehearse.

00:04:56.628 --> 00:04:58.430
-We do have a saying
in the Foo Fighters

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:02.034
that, "If it gets any better,
it's gonna get worse."

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:04.203
So we -- Actually,
I don't like to rehearse,

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:05.938
because I like
to get out on stage

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:08.007
and just have it
be raw and real,

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:10.042
so it's not like
this big choreographed thing.

00:05:10.042 --> 00:05:11.477
So when we get up there,

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.413
it's, like, that moment,
and it's super real.

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.014
-That's wild.
-Yeah.

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:17.316
-Were you always like that?
I mean, you have --

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:19.952
There's another series
based on a book

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:23.255
that your mom wrote called
"From Cradle to Stage."

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:26.258
-Yes.
-Basically raising a rock star.

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:28.828
-Yeah, my mom --
Okay, so my mother was

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.297
a public-school teacher
for 35 years,

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:33.065
and when she retired, she --

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.267
I was like, "Mom, don't do
that cruise-ship thing."

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:37.503
Like, you know, like retirees.
I was like --

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:40.172
I was like, "I got a cruise
ship. It's a tour bus.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:41.741
Like, let's go."
And she just jumped --

00:05:41.741 --> 00:05:43.309
And she started
going on tour with me.

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.611
And I gave her a laminate,
and she would just go hang out.

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:47.546
I'd be on stage playing.
I'd get off stage like,

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:49.649
"Where's my mom?
Where's my mom?"

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.283
And she'd be like, you know,

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:53.653
drinking beers
with Green Day upstairs.

00:05:53.653 --> 00:05:54.887
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.689
-Yeah, mom!
-She's very cool.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:59.392
She's very cool.

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:02.194
And so -- But then, when she
was on tour, she was like,

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:03.496
"Where's all the other moms?"

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:05.498
Like, "Where are
all the other moms?"

00:06:05.498 --> 00:06:08.468
So she went to go find
these mothers to interview them

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:11.203
and talk to them about what
it's like to raise a musician.

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:13.271
So, in the book,
she talked to Dr. Dre,

00:06:13.271 --> 00:06:14.674
and she talked to Mike D,

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:16.209
and she talked
to Pharrell's mom.

00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:18.977
And she talked to Zac Brown's
mom, Miranda Lambert's mom.

00:06:18.977 --> 00:06:20.880
And then --
So she made this great book.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.681
And then we did
a documentary series,

00:06:22.681 --> 00:06:24.617
and that's the thing
that's on TV now.

00:06:24.617 --> 00:06:25.985
It's awesome.

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:28.020
-That's super cool.
And I didn't bring it up,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.722
but congrats
on being inducted into

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:31.624
the Rock &amp; Roll
Hall of Fame -- again.

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:35.361
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:39.231
Mom, you did pretty well!
You did a good job, Mom!

00:06:39.231 --> 00:06:40.733
Here's a clip.

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:42.501
Here's Dave
and his mom, Virginia,

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:44.337
in "From Cradle to Stage."

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:45.871
Take a look.

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:48.207
-When I saw a Nirvana show

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:50.376
where you destroyed
all the instruments

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:53.579
at the end of it,
I was aghast.

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.147
♪♪

00:06:55.147 --> 00:06:58.417
After growing up
with not more than 10 cents

00:06:58.417 --> 00:07:01.687
left over each month,
I thought you knew

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:06.058
that one should not waste
valuable materials.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:08.161
♪♪

00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:10.997
-Uh, first of all,
I didn't start it.

00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:12.798
[ Laughter ]

00:07:12.798 --> 00:07:15.434
-I know. Okay.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:18.871
-Yeah. You just finished it.
That's all. You got a point.

